===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|greve|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|græfe|grǣfe|lang=ang}}, {{term|græfe|grǣfa|bush, bramble, grove, thicket, copse, brush-wood (for burning), fuel|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|grainiz|twig|lang=en}}, of unknown origin. Cognate with {{etyl|non|-}} {{term|grein||branch, bough|lang=non}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|graf|grāf|lang=ang}}, {{term|grafa|grāfa|grove|lang=ang}}. See {{l|en|grove}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{obsolete}} A [[bush]]; a [[tree]]; a [[grove]].
# {{obsolete}} A [[bough]]; a [[branch]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|greve|lang=enm}}, {{term|greyve|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|græfa|grǣfa|lang=ang}}, {{term|grefa|grēfa|pit, cave, hole, grave, trench|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|grōbō|pit, ditch|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|gʰrebʰ-|to dig, scratch, scrape|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|frr|-}} {{term|groop||pit, sewer, gutter|lang=frr}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|groef||pit, hole, gutter|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|Grube||pit, hole|lang=de}}, {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|gröf||pit, grave|lang=is}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{obsolete}} A [[ditch]] or [[trench]].

===Etymology 3===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|greve|lang=enm}}, {{term|grayve|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|fro}} {{term|greve||shin|lang=fro}}, of unknown origin.

